PS Vita in-app self-update (app/src/utils/vita_install.cpp, issue #14)
  The technique used to install a homebrew VPK from within the app — forging a
  self-signed fake-package "head.bin" and promoting the extracted directory with
  ScePromoterUtility — is the de-facto interoperability standard shared by every
  PS Vita homebrew installer, originating from the HENkaku / molecularShell
  lineage. The canonical reference implementation is VitaShell by TheOfficialFloW
  (https://github.com/TheOfficialFloW/VitaShell), licensed under the GNU General
  Public License v3.0.

  The C++ in vita_install.cpp is an independent reimplementation. The fake-package
  header template embedded in app/src/utils/vita_head_bin.h is copied verbatim
  from VitaShell's resources/head.bin (a fixed functional artifact the patched
  system promoter requires; sha256
  cbb88299319048e19115a1fc9c76b04e33745291749de0e08963b9b425623f45).

  The PS Vita build links a GPL build of libmpv, so the distributed pleNx.vpk is
  already a GPL combined work; reusing this GPLv3-derived material in that binary
  is licence-compatible. This attribution is provided in recognition of that
  origin.

Inter (resources/font/inter.ttf, inter_medium.ttf, inter_semibold.ttf,
       inter_bold.ttf)
  Copyright (c) 2016 The Inter Project Authors (https://github.com/rsms/inter),
  licensed under the SIL Open Font License 1.1 (https://openfontlicense.org).
  Obtained from Google Fonts. It is the app's primary text face, chosen to match
  NuvioTV, which uses Inter as its default. The Medium (500), SemiBold (600) and
  Bold (700) cuts are static instances of the same upstream variable font,
  produced with fontTools' instancer, so that the UI can use the weights
  NuvioTV's Material3 type scale calls for.

  Every shipped cut differs from the upstream build in one respect: its Private
  Use Area cmap entries (U+E000–U+F8FF, 745 mappings to internal alternates such
  as `four.numr`) are stripped. borealis addresses controller button glyphs at
  U+E0E0–U+E0F0, and a primary font claiming that range shadows the keymap font
  that actually draws them — with them present, "B Back" rendered as "5 Back".
  The alternates remain reachable through the font's OpenType features, which is
  how they are meant to be selected; only the PUA shortcut is removed.
